  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1249 PM MDT Mon Sep 21 2026\n\nCurrent WV imagery shows mid-latitude low moving in over SoCal with \nnarrow band of moisture streaming into it over Arizona.\nFurther south, plentiful sub-tropical moisture is present, both due \nto old cut off low just southwest of the southern Baja, and from \nnewly formed TS Polo. At the surface, a weak back door cool front \nhas moved into NE New Mexico. \n\nFor today/tonight...should remain relatively quiet. Plenty of CU \ndevelopment next 3 hours. HRRR showing a few random showers or \nthunderstorms possible this afternoon. By late tonight, toward \nmidnight, the back door front reaches the eastern CWA and into the\ncentral CWA after midnight. HRRR showing isolated shower/storm \nactivity RG Valley late night. \n\nTuesday through Thursday...Pacific upper low/trough has now moved in \nover the Great Basin and rapidly draws in the sub-tropical moisture \nwith the help of a developing rather strong sub-tropical jet and a \npossible short wave/vort max moving up from northern Mexico. PWs by \nTuesday night approach 1.5 or 1.6 inches in the central and eastern \nCWA, which is near record highs for this time of the year. Expect \nscattered showers and thunderstorms early Tuesday afternoon and just \ncontinuing through the night into Wednesday morning. A Flood Watch \nis in effect for much of the CWA Tuesday into Wednesday. The far \nsouthwest is not included at this time but I may put in with the \nnext issuance. Wednesday/Wednesday night is still a bit uncertain as \na lot depends on how much cloud cover persists Wednesday morning. \nBut still expect numerous showers/thunderstorms. Widespread \nshowers/thunderstorms to continue Thursday into early Friday \nmorning. \n\nFriday through Sunday...sub-tropical high well established over \nsouthern Texas and begins ridging west across northern Mexico.\nThis will tend to reduce the moist southerly flow. PWs drop \nsignificantly, ranging from around .6 inches north to 1.1 inch far \nsoutheast. Thus expect showers/storms to become more isolated, with \nthe risk of flooding lowered significantly (antecedent moisture \nissues could continue some flood risk). Cut off low from the \noriginal Pacific trough then moves across the area Friday night. \nLower PWs and dewpoints suggest limited activity Saturday, though \nPWs begin rebounding again Sunday. Also moderate moisture gradient \nwith higher values in northern Mexico could increase activity if it \nmoves further north than planned. One significant change in the \nmodels; neither GFS/ECMWF are now bringing any tropical storm \nremains to our area through the 10 day forecast period.",
